This replaces the version in the queue (sha256 a76004bde0ec...).  Two
things changed:

- iattr_seq moved from beside kernfs_iattr_rwsem to the end of struct
  kernfs_root, cacheline aligned.  Only fs/kernfs/kernfs-internal.h
  differs from the old diff.
- The changelog is rewritten around testbot's 64-CPU results.

Base: mm-new 3d27e49eb905 ("tools/mm: page-types: flush pending idle bitmap
bits after walking").  Built: fs/kernfs/ with x86_64 defconfig and gcc
13.3.0, no warnings.  Nothing was booted or run on this version.

Layout with that config: kernfs_iattr_rwsem at offset 136, the last of the
other fields ends at 232, iattr_seq is at 256, sizeof is 320.

Where the figures in the changelog come from.  All are from testbot's
report of 26 November, from runs of the old version on mm-new without
Shakeel Butt's six patches:

  lookups, perf profile, chown race  kernfs-rcu-warm-redo-64cpu-20261125-01
  stat()/access() interference       kernfs-rcu-contention-kcsan-selinux-
                                     20261126-01
  sysfs reads, cgroup file reads     kernfs-rcu-workloads-64cpu-20261125-01
  64 processes opening one file      kernfs-rcu-perf-64cpu-20261125-01

The 77,677 and 0 with the test-only delays are from the old version's own
notes (four emulated CPUs).  I did not rerun them.  Replace the figures
with those from the retest below.

Things you should know before merging:

- The seqcount is not where testbot's experiment had it.  That experiment
  aligned it in place, which going by the offsets in its report (0xc0 of
  0x140) leaves kernfs_supers_rwsem, and probably kernfs_rename_lock, on
  the same cacheline.  Node removal and kernfs_notify() take the first
  for reading and kernfs path lookups (/proc/PID/cgroup) take the second,
  so they would write that line.  Testbot's interference test had only
  stat() callers and could not show it.  I have not seen its patch; this
  is inferred.  Step 2 below tests the new placement.

- The alignment relies on kmalloc() returning 64-byte aligned memory for
  a 320-byte object, which the kmalloc-512 cache does.  Where it did not,
  the cost would be the false sharing again, not a bug.

- Shakeel's "kernfs: stop taking kernfs_iattr_rwsem for writing in
  mkdir/rmdir" [2] was applied to vfs-7.5.kernfs by Christian on 20
  November.  As of 25 November it is not in the published branch
  (ccf2a4692fed) and not in mm-new.  The old version of this patch
  applies on top of the six with offsets, and fs/kernfs/ builds there.
  On top of them the changelog paragraph about times and link count is
  no longer needed, because the refresh stops copying them.

- The kfree_rcu() in kernfs_kill_sb() is still there by reading.  Testbot
  ran that path 2,216 times under KASAN without a report, which shows
  that it runs, not that a walker was ever inside the window.

- Testbot ran these on the old version, A against B, with nothing wrong:
  the permission, negative dentry and chown race tests at full speed,
  lockdep with KASAN, PREEMPT_RT, a veth rename loop and a Debian boot.
  Not tested: an SELinux decision on a kernfs path (only the mode bits,
  with SELinux enforcing), 32-bit, any other architecture, bare metal.

- KCSAN reported races on both kernels in generic VFS code (d_flags,
  d_inode, i_flags).  None named a field which this patch adds or reads
  locklessly.  The sets of reports differed between A and B, and testbot
  does not call either kernel clean.

- None of the people in the Cc list has seen this.  Shakeel wrote in
  8c2a5e65a977 that lifting the LOOKUP_RCU bail "is left to the series
  fixing" kernfs_iop_permission(), so he should see it first.

- The history and the references [1] to [11] are carried over unchanged
  from the old version.  Of those I have read only [2].

FOR THE TESTING CHAT

Work in steps and look at each result before starting the next.  Do not
leave a list of runs going unattended.

Kernels:

  A   mm-new 3d27e49eb905 plus Shakeel Butt's six kernfs patches:
        b4 am 20261118220154.2965621-1-shakeel.butt@linux.dev
      then git am the result.  It applies cleanly.
  B   A plus this patch (it applies with offsets).

  timing pair: no LOCK_STAT, LOCKDEP, PROVE_LOCKING, KASAN or KCSAN.
               Check that CONFIG_LOCKDEP is not set before timing.
  debug pair:  PROVE_LOCKING, PROVE_RCU, KASAN, DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP.

Guest: 64 CPUs, KVM, as before.  No memory limits, no swap, no probes
while timing.

Setup, as root, cgroup2 on /sys/fs/cgroup, /tmp on tmpfs:

  gcc -O2 -static -o kt kt.c                 (kt.c is below, unchanged)
  mkdir -p /sys/fs/cgroup/t/a/b/c/d /sys/fs/cgroup/work /sys/fs/cgroup/v
  for i in $(seq 0 63); do mkdir -p /sys/fs/cgroup/t/p$i; done
  for i in $(seq 0 31); do mkdir -p /sys/fs/cgroup/u/q$i; done
  mkdir -p /tmp/t/a/b/c/d; echo 1 > /tmp/t/a/b/c/d/f

Path sets:

  C1  /sys/fs/cgroup/t/a/b/c/d/cgroup.procs
  CN  /sys/fs/cgroup/t/p*/cgroup.procs                 (64 paths)
  S1  /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/topology/core_id
  SN  /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/topology/core_id    (64 paths)
  T1  /tmp/t/a/b/c/d/f                                 (control)
  UN  /sys/fs/cgroup/u/q*/cgroup.procs                 (32 paths)

1. Throughput, timing pair.  These figures go into the changelog.

     for OP in access, open, stat; NPROC in 1, 64;
     PATHS in C1, CN, S1, SN, T1:
         kt perf OP NPROC 5 PATHS

   Three runs of each per boot, two boots per kernel, order A B A B.
   Report ops/s as median [min-max] for A and B and the change.
   Expect B well above A on the kernfs rows at 64 processes, except
   open on C1 and S1, and T1 the same on both.
   Flag any kernfs row where B is below A by more than the run-to-run
   range.  Flag T1 if A and B differ by more than its range.

2. Interference, timing pair, B and then A.  Three 5-second runs each of

     a)  kt perf access 32 5 CN
     b)  a) while "kt perf stat 32 20 UN" runs in the background
     c)  a) while four shell loops each mkdir and rmdir their own
         directory under /sys/fs/cgroup/v
     d)  a) while eight shell loops each read /proc/self/cgroup

   Start the background load two seconds before a).  Report the access
   ops/s of each, and for c) and d) how many loop iterations ran.
   Expect on B: b), c) and d) each within 10% of a).
   Flag on B anything more than 20% below a), and report A's figures
   beside it whatever they are.

3. File reads from different cgroups, timing pair.  The earlier cgroup
   read mixes had every worker read the same files in one directory.
   Rerun the below-like full-to-EOF mix from kernfs-rcu-workloads-64cpu
   with 64 workers, each reading the files of its own cgroup
   (/sys/fs/cgroup/t/p0 to p63), and once more with the shared directory
   as before.  Report A and B for both.  This is a measurement, with no
   expected result; say what limits it if perf can tell.

4. Correctness, both pairs, A and B, nothing else running:

     kt perm /sys/fs/cgroup/work 300      expect "0 wrong"
     kt neg  /sys/fs/cgroup/work 3000     expect "0 wrong"
     kt race /sys/fs/cgroup/work 60       expect "0 ALLOWED, 0 other"

   then on the debug pair

     kt churn /sys/fs/cgroup/work 60
     kt mnt /m 60                         (mkdir /m first)

   and read dmesg.  Report the chown and lookup counts of kt race.
   Flag any other result and say on which kernel.  Flag any lockdep,
   KASAN, RCU or "sleeping function called from invalid context" report
   which A does not also give.

5. The veth rename loop from the warm-redo case, 60 seconds on the
   timing pair and on the debug pair.  Every stat must succeed or fail
   with ENOENT.

Not wanted this time: reclaim, KCSAN, SELinux, PREEMPT_RT, distro boot.
The code those exercised is unchanged; only a struct field moved.

The test program, kt.c:

:// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
:/*
: * kt - kernfs path lookup tests
: *
: * kt perf OP NPROC SECS PATH...
: *	NPROC processes repeat OP (stat, access or open) for SECS seconds.
: *	Process i uses PATH[i % npaths].  Prints operations per second.
: *
: * kt perm DIR ITERS
: *	chmod() and chown() the cgroup directory DIR and check after each
: *	change that an unprivileged process sees exactly the new permission.
: *
: * kt race DIR SECS
: *	Flip the owner of DIR between (1000,2000) and (2000,1000), mode
: *	0007.  uid 1000/gid 1000 is denied in both states and allowed only
: *	if it sees a half-made change.  Counts lookups which were allowed.
: *
: * kt neg DIR ITERS
: *	mkdir()/rmdir() DIR/x and check that a lookup sees each change.
: *
: * kt churn DIR SECS
: *	mkdir/rmdir, chmod, chown, stat and access under DIR from several
: *	processes at once, for debug kernels.  Checks nothing itself.
: *
: * kt mnt MNT SECS
: *	Repeatedly mount sysfs on MNT from a new network namespace and
: *	lazily unmount it, while other processes walk into it.  For debug
: *	kernels: each round frees a superblock under the walkers.
: */
:#define _GNU_SOURCE
:#include <errno.h>
:#include <fcntl.h>
:#include <grp.h>
:#include <sched.h>
:#include <signal.h>
:#include <stdio.h>
:#include <stdlib.h>
:#include <string.h>
:#include <time.h>
:#include <unistd.h>
:#include <sys/mman.h>
:#include <sys/mount.h>
:#include <sys/stat.h>
:#include <sys/wait.h>
:
:static double now(void)
:{
:	struct timespec ts;
:
:	clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
:	return ts.tv_sec + ts.tv_nsec / 1e9;
:}
:
:static void die(const char *what)
:{
:	perror(what);
:	exit(2);
:}
:
:struct shared {
:	volatile int go;
:	volatile int stop;
:	volatile unsigned long count[64 * 8];	/* one cacheline apart */
:	volatile unsigned long allowed;
:	volatile unsigned long denied;
:	volatile unsigned long other;
:};
:
:static struct shared *sh;
:
:static void drop_to(uid_t uid, gid_t gid)
:{
:	if (setgroups(0, NULL) || setgid(gid) || setuid(uid))
:		die("drop privileges");
:}
:
:static int do_perf(int argc, char **argv)
:{
:	const char *op = argv[0];
:	int nproc = atoi(argv[1]);
:	double secs = atof(argv[2]);
:	char **paths = argv + 3;
:	int npaths = argc - 3;
:	unsigned long total = 0;
:	double t0, t1;
:	int i;
:
:	if (npaths < 1 || nproc < 1 || nproc > 64)
:		return 2;
:
:	for (i = 0; i < nproc; i++) {
:		pid_t pid = fork();
:
:		if (pid < 0)
:			die("fork");
:		if (!pid) {
:			const char *path = paths[i % npaths];
:			volatile unsigned long *cnt = &sh->count[i * 8];
:			unsigned long n = 0;
:			struct stat st;
:			int fd;
:
:			while (!sh->go)
:				;
:			while (!sh->stop) {
:				if (op[0] == 's') {
:					if (stat(path, &st))
:						die(path);
:				} else if (op[0] == 'a') {
:					if (access(path, R_OK))
:						die(path);
:				} else {
:					fd = open(path, O_RDONLY);
:					if (fd < 0)
:						die(path);
:					close(fd);
:				}
:				*cnt = ++n;
:			}
:			_exit(0);
:		}
:	}
:
:	usleep(200000);
:	t0 = now();
:	sh->go = 1;
:	usleep((useconds_t)(secs * 1e6));
:	sh->stop = 1;
:	t1 = now();
:	for (i = 0; i < nproc; i++)
:		total += sh->count[i * 8];
:	while (wait(NULL) > 0)
:		;
:	printf("perf %s nproc=%d npaths=%d: %.0f ops/s\n", op, nproc, npaths,
:	       total / (t1 - t0));
:	return 0;
:}
:
:/* Run access(path) as uid/gid 1000 in a child; return 0 or errno. */
:static int access_as_user(const char *path)
:{
:	int status;
:	pid_t pid = fork();
:
:	if (pid < 0)
:		die("fork");
:	if (!pid) {
:		drop_to(1000, 1000);
:		_exit(access(path, F_OK) ? errno : 0);
:	}
:	waitpid(pid, &status, 0);
:	return WEXITSTATUS(status);
:}
:
:/* Keep the walk through @path hot in other processes. */
:static void start_walkers(const char *path, int n, int as_user)
:{
:	int i;
:
:	for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
:		pid_t pid = fork();
:
:		if (pid < 0)
:			die("fork");
:		if (!pid) {
:			if (as_user)
:				drop_to(1000, 1000);
:			while (!sh->stop) {
:				if (!access(path, F_OK))
:					__sync_fetch_and_add(&sh->allowed, 1);
:				else if (errno == EACCES)
:					__sync_fetch_and_add(&sh->denied, 1);
:				else
:					__sync_fetch_and_add(&sh->other, 1);
:			}
:			_exit(0);
:		}
:	}
:}
:
:static void stop_walkers(void)
:{
:	sh->stop = 1;
:	while (wait(NULL) > 0)
:		;
:}
:
:static int do_perm(int argc, char **argv)
:{
:	const char *dir = argv[0];
:	int iters = atoi(argv[1]);
:	static const struct {
:		uid_t uid;
:		gid_t gid;
:		mode_t mode;
:		int want;
:	} steps[] = {
:		{ 0,    0,    0755, 0 },
:		{ 0,    0,    0700, EACCES },
:		{ 1000, 0,    0700, 0 },
:		{ 1000, 0,    0070, EACCES },
:		{ 0,    1000, 0070, 0 },
:		{ 0,    1000, 0007, EACCES },
:		{ 0,    0,    0007, 0 },
:		{ 0,    0,    0750, EACCES },
:		{ 2000, 1000, 0750, 0 },
:		{ 2000, 2000, 0750, EACCES },
:	};
:	unsigned long checks = 0, wrong = 0;
:	char path[512];
:	int i, s, got;
:
:	snprintf(path, sizeof(path), "%s/cgroup.procs", dir);
:	start_walkers(path, 2, 1);
:	start_walkers(path, 1, 0);
:
:	for (i = 0; i < iters; i++) {
:		for (s = 0; s < (int)(sizeof(steps) / sizeof(steps[0])); s++) {
:			if (chown(dir, steps[s].uid, steps[s].gid))
:				die("chown");
:			if (chmod(dir, steps[s].mode))
:				die("chmod");
:			got = access_as_user(path);
:			checks++;
:			if (got != steps[s].want) {
:				wrong++;
:				printf("perm: iter %d step %d: got %d want %d\n",
:				       i, s, got, steps[s].want);
:			}
:		}
:	}
:	stop_walkers();
:	if (chown(dir, 0, 0))
:		die("chown");
:	chmod(dir, 0755);
:	printf("perm: %lu checks, %lu wrong (walkers: %lu allowed, %lu denied, %lu other)\n",
:	       checks, wrong, sh->allowed, sh->denied, sh->other);
:	return wrong != 0;
